# Kavacha patch 0038 — Per-Space containers become opt-in, default OFF
# (ROADMAP Phase 2 "Cross-workspace identity"; user decision 2026-07-31).
#
# WHAT: a new pref kavacha.workspaces.isolate-containers (default FALSE, set in
# ui/defaults/kavacha-ux.js) gates whether a template-created Space gets its own
# Firefox container, plus a "Space isolation" checkbox in the Workspaces
# Settings pane (patch 0032) that drives it.
#
# WHY: patch 0006's templates each forced a dedicated container. Containers are
# separate FIRST-PARTY cookie jars, so a Google / GitHub / Slack sign-in in one
# Space did not carry into another and the user re-authenticated per Space.
# Defaulting off restores "sign in once, signed in everywhere" — which is what
# people actually mean when they ask for Chrome-style Google sign-in (that is
# cookie SSO across *.google.com, not Chrome Sync; see the ROADMAP Phase 5 note
# for why Google account sync itself is ruled out).
#
# SECURITY: defaulting off costs no tracking protection. Firefox's Total Cookie
# Protection (network.cookie.cookieBehavior=5, privacy/tracker-controls/kavacha.js)
# already partitions THIRD-PARTY state by top-level site in every Space,
# container or not. A container's unique contribution is a separate first-party
# session — i.e. two accounts on the same site at once. That capability is what
# the pref restores when turned on, and nothing else is lost by it being off.
# This is consistent with ADR 0005, which already established that Spaces are an
# organizational boundary and not a privacy boundary.
#
# EXEMPTION: the "privacy" template ALWAYS gets a container regardless of the
# pref. Isolation is the only thing that template promises; honouring the pref
# there would make it lie. Keyed on template.id so no template-table edit is
# needed.
#
# NON-RETROACTIVE: the pref governs Space CREATION only. Existing Spaces keep
# whatever containerTabId they already hold — clearing it would orphan the
# cookies inside that container and read to the user as being signed out
# everywhere. Turning the pref on later likewise affects only new Spaces.
#
# NOTE: validated by git-apply against browser/zen-upstream; both touched JS
# STATUS 2026-08-01: the 'no local Zen checkout / NOT BUILD-VERIFIED' caveat below is
# STALE and retired -- browser/zen-upstream IS checked out and the full chain
# builds, packages and launches. What remains is functional (L4) verification.
# L4 verified 2026-08-01: all three container arms + live pref observer.
# Authoritative per-patch status: documentation/VERIFICATION.md section 3.
#
# (historical) files pass `node --check`. NOT yet build- or Marionette-verified — see
# documentation/VERIFICATION.md. L4 test: with the pref off, create a Student
# Space and confirm containerTabId is 0 and a google.com login carries into it;
# create a Private Space and confirm it still gets its own container; flip the
# checkbox on and confirm a new Student Space gets one.
#
# Touches: src/zen/spaces/ZenSpaceManager.mjs (gate the container creation),
# src/browser/components/preferences/kavachaWorkspaces.inc.xhtml (checkbox),
# src/browser/components/preferences/kavacha-workspaces.js (pref wiring),
# locales/en-US/browser/browser/preferences/zen-preferences.ftl (strings).
# The pref DEFAULT lives in ui/defaults/kavacha-ux.js (repo-level, appended to
# branding prefs by build/generate-branding.sh) — not in this patch.
# Applies on top of 0037.
